{
  "dom_id": "step-e6112",
  "name": "Example: Determining the Units of a Derivative",
  "problem": "A rocket is at a distance of $r(t)$ meters from the ground $t$ seconds after it is launched. What are the units of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}?$",
  "worked_solution": "We have that the height $r(t)$ is measured in meters and the time is measured in seconds. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t): & \\text{meters} \\\\ t: & \\text{seconds}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$ measures a change in $r(t)$ over a change in $t.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}: & \\frac{\\text{meters}}{\\text{seconds}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t}$ is measured in \"meters per second\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
